1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,216
Behind a mirror in a small office near Times Square, a camera is running.

2
00:00:06,216 --> 00:00:10,074
On the other side of the glass, two men sit at a desk.

3
00:00:10,525 --> 00:00:14,981
One is a German-born engineer who calls himself Harry Sawyer.

4
00:00:14,981 --> 00:00:20,751
The other is sixty-three years old, and he has been a spy for most of his life.

5
00:00:21,202 --> 00:00:23,407
His name is Frederick Duquesne.

6
00:00:23,407 --> 00:00:32,582
He has fought the British in South Africa, escaped from their prison camps, and worked for German intelligence in two world wars.

7
00:00:33,031 --> 00:00:35,386
He has used about thirty names.

8
00:00:35,386 --> 00:00:37,209
He trusts almost nobody.

9
00:00:37,209 --> 00:00:42,298
And he is talking, on film, to the Federal Bureau of Investigation.

10
00:00:42,749 --> 00:00:47,697
Within days of this picture, thirty-three people would be in custody.

11
00:00:47,697 --> 00:00:49,920
All of them would be convicted.

12
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:56,518
The FBI still calls it the largest espionage case in American history to end in convictions.

13
00:00:56,518 --> 00:01:00,678
Their sentences added up to more than three hundred years.

14
00:01:01,128 --> 00:01:07,413
But there is a detail in the file that the famous version of this story tends to skip.

15
00:01:07,413 --> 00:01:18,740
The most valuable American secret this network ever stole had already left New York, hidden inside an umbrella, two years before the FBI's case even began.

16
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:19,909
Hold on to that.

17
00:01:20,359 --> 00:01:24,013
So this is not really the story of a secret saved.

18
00:01:24,013 --> 00:01:26,790
It is the story of something stranger.

19
00:01:27,239 --> 00:01:36,234
How the FBI, for the first time, took over an enemy spy service's own line of communication, and ran it for sixteen months.

20
00:01:36,234 --> 00:01:41,499
And how the man who made that possible was almost erased from the story.

21
00:01:41,948 --> 00:01:44,197
This is The Defector Files.

22
00:01:45,847 --> 00:01:51,337
The file begins with a man named Gottlieb Adolf Wilhelm Sebold.

23
00:01:51,337 --> 00:01:56,827
He was born in Mülheim, in the Rhineland, on March tenth, 1899.

24
00:01:56,827 --> 00:02:02,753
He served in the German army's engineers during the First World War.

25
00:02:03,203 --> 00:02:16,054
In 1922 he emigrated to the United States, and over the next years he worked in industrial plants and aircraft factories, in America and in South America.

26
00:02:16,503 --> 00:02:21,191
On February tenth, 1936, he became an American citizen.

27
00:02:21,191 --> 00:02:22,811
He went by William.

28
00:02:22,811 --> 00:02:27,413
He had a wife, Helen, and a life in the United States.

29
00:02:27,864 --> 00:02:34,393
In February 1939 he sailed back to Germany to visit his mother in Mülheim.

30
00:02:34,393 --> 00:02:37,393
It was meant to be a family visit.

31
00:02:37,393 --> 00:02:39,158
It did not stay one.

32
00:02:39,608 --> 00:02:44,522
When his ship docked in Hamburg, a man from the Gestapo approached him.

33
00:02:44,522 --> 00:02:53,866
According to the FBI's account, the man told Sebold that he would be contacted, because of what he knew about American aircraft plants.

34
00:02:54,317 --> 00:02:55,703
Then he let him go.

35
00:02:55,703 --> 00:02:59,424
Sebold traveled on to Mülheim and found work there.

36
00:02:59,875 --> 00:03:06,616
In September 1939, the month the war began in Europe, a visitor came to see him.

37
00:03:06,616 --> 00:03:09,397
He called himself Doctor Gassner.

38
00:03:09,848 --> 00:03:16,109
He asked detailed questions about military aircraft and equipment in the United States.

39
00:03:16,109 --> 00:03:17,692
Then he made an offer.

40
00:03:17,692 --> 00:03:22,443
Sebold should go back to America and work for German intelligence.

41
00:03:22,893 --> 00:03:29,905
When Sebold hesitated, Gassner and a second man, who called himself Doctor Renken, used leverage.

42
00:03:29,905 --> 00:03:38,218
Years earlier Sebold had served time in a German jail, and he had left that off his American naturalization papers.

43
00:03:38,669 --> 00:03:42,489
If he refused, they would make sure the Americans found out.

44
00:03:42,489 --> 00:03:45,163
There were threats against his family too.

45
00:03:45,612 --> 00:03:47,793
Doctor Renken was not a doctor.

46
00:03:47,793 --> 00:03:57,290
He was Major Nikolaus Ritter of the Abwehr, the German military intelligence service, head of its air intelligence work out of Hamburg.

47
00:03:57,741 --> 00:04:02,121
Ritter had lived in New York for years in the nineteen twenties.

48
00:04:02,121 --> 00:04:05,750
He knew America, and he had already run agents there.

49
00:04:06,199 --> 00:04:07,212
Sebold agreed.

50
00:04:07,212 --> 00:04:11,986
He was sent to a training school in Hamburg for about seven weeks.

51
00:04:11,986 --> 00:04:21,461
He learned to work a clandestine shortwave radio, to use codes, and to read microphotographs, messages shrunk to the size of a dot.

52
00:04:21,911 --> 00:04:29,365
He was given a cover name, Harry Sawyer, a code name, TRAMP, and an Abwehr number, A three five four nine.

53
00:04:29,815 --> 00:04:35,597
And then, before he left Germany, he did something the Abwehr never found out about.

54
00:04:35,597 --> 00:04:42,411
He went to the American consulate in Cologne, and he insisted on seeing the Consul General himself.

55
00:04:42,860 --> 00:04:45,190
He said he had been blackmailed.

56
00:04:45,190 --> 00:04:48,101
He said he was a loyal American citizen.

57
00:04:48,101 --> 00:04:51,232
And he said he wanted to work with the FBI.

58
00:04:51,682 --> 00:04:57,293
Here the file invites a question, and this is interpretation rather than fact.

59
00:04:57,293 --> 00:05:01,680
Why did the Abwehr trust a man it had recruited by blackmail?

60
00:05:01,680 --> 00:05:04,413
Coercion can force someone to say yes.

61
00:05:04,413 --> 00:05:06,284
It cannot make them loyal.

62
00:05:06,733 --> 00:05:18,326
Ritter had built his operation on a recruit with every reason to hate him, and then sent that recruit to a country where he had a home, a wife and a citizenship to protect.

63
00:05:19,975 --> 00:05:26,657
Sebold sailed from Genoa and reached New York on February eighth, 1940.

64
00:05:26,657 --> 00:05:28,538
The FBI was waiting.

65
00:05:28,988 --> 00:05:43,738
Over the following weeks, agents and Sebold built a cover for Harry Sawyer: a consulting diesel engineer, with his own firm, the Diesel Research Company, and an office in the Times Square district of Manhattan.

66
00:05:44,189 --> 00:05:45,914
The office was a stage.

67
00:05:45,914 --> 00:05:48,164
There were hidden microphones.

68
00:05:48,164 --> 00:05:56,791
One wall held a mirror that was really a window, and in the room behind it FBI agents could watch, listen and film.

69
00:05:56,791 --> 00:06:02,266
Everything a German agent said at Sawyer's desk could be shown to a jury.

70
00:06:02,716 --> 00:06:05,431
The case went to the top of the Bureau.

71
00:06:05,431 --> 00:06:06,197
Director J.

72
00:06:06,197 --> 00:06:11,835
Edgar Hoover followed it closely, and briefed President Franklin Roosevelt on it.

73
00:06:12,285 --> 00:06:19,054
Special Agent James Ellsworth was assigned to stay with Sebold for the entire investigation.

74
00:06:19,054 --> 00:06:24,646
For sixteen months, he would shadow almost every move the double agent made.

75
00:06:25,096 --> 00:06:28,114
The second part of the plan was the radio.

76
00:06:28,114 --> 00:06:33,360
Ritter had told Sebold to set up a shortwave station and contact Hamburg.

77
00:06:33,810 --> 00:06:39,772
So in May 1940, on Long Island, FBI laboratory engineers built one.

78
00:06:39,772 --> 00:06:44,399
The men who tapped out the messages were FBI agents.

79
00:06:44,399 --> 00:06:49,115
To the Abwehr, it was TRAMP's transmitter in America.

80
00:06:49,564 --> 00:06:58,127
Over the next sixteen months, that station became one of the main channels between German agents in New York and their headquarters.

81
00:06:58,579 --> 00:07:05,626
The FBI says it sent more than three hundred messages to Germany and received about two hundred.

82
00:07:05,626 --> 00:07:08,489
Everything going out was checked first.

83
00:07:08,489 --> 00:07:10,251
Some of it was harmless.

84
00:07:10,251 --> 00:07:11,792
Some of it was false.

85
00:07:12,242 --> 00:07:17,600
This is the part of the case that matters most, and it is easy to miss.

86
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:20,468
The FBI was not simply watching spies.

87
00:07:20,468 --> 00:07:23,259
It had become the spies' post office.

88
00:07:23,709 --> 00:07:30,789
Every German agent who wanted a report to reach Hamburg quickly had a reason to bring it to Sawyer.

89
00:07:30,789 --> 00:07:33,507
The trap did not need to chase anyone.

90
00:07:33,507 --> 00:07:35,152
It only needed to wait.

91
00:07:36,802 --> 00:07:41,919
One of the first names on Sebold's list was a code name: DUNN.

92
00:07:41,919 --> 00:07:45,468
Ritter wanted him to find DUNN in New York.

93
00:07:45,468 --> 00:07:48,439
DUNN was Frederick Joubert Duquesne.

94
00:07:48,439 --> 00:07:56,609
He was born in the Cape Colony, in South Africa, on September twenty-first, 1877, to a Boer family.

95
00:07:57,059 --> 00:08:01,040
His own account of his life reads like an adventure serial.

96
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:04,009
He fought for the Boers against the British.

97
00:08:04,009 --> 00:08:10,892
He said he was captured by the British three times and by the Portuguese once, and escaped every time.

98
00:08:11,341 --> 00:08:20,800
He was shipped as a prisoner of war to Bermuda, and got away to the United States, where he became a citizen in 1913.

99
00:08:21,250 --> 00:08:30,027
In New York he wrote for the New York Herald, lectured on big game hunting, and claimed to have taught Theodore Roosevelt to shoot.

100
00:08:30,476 --> 00:08:41,663
In 1910 he was even part of a serious proposal to bring hippopotamuses into the Louisiana bayous, to ease an American meat shortage.

101
00:08:41,663 --> 00:08:44,715
That one, remarkably, is documented.

102
00:08:45,164 --> 00:08:46,845
Other parts are not.

103
00:08:46,845 --> 00:08:55,921
His most famous boast was that in 1916 he had sunk the British cruiser Hampshire with Lord Kitchener aboard.

104
00:08:55,921 --> 00:09:01,130
A 1932 biography was even titled The Man Who Killed Kitchener.

105
00:09:01,581 --> 00:09:03,499
The record points elsewhere.

106
00:09:03,499 --> 00:09:07,267
The Hampshire struck a mine laid by a German submarine.

107
00:09:07,267 --> 00:09:11,446
There is no good evidence that Duquesne was anywhere near it.

108
00:09:11,895 --> 00:09:14,502
So the file has to separate two men.

109
00:09:14,502 --> 00:09:20,077
The legend, which Duquesne wrote himself and told to anyone who would listen.

110
00:09:20,077 --> 00:09:23,552
And the record, which is smaller but still real.

111
00:09:24,003 --> 00:09:31,166
In the First World War he did work for Germany, against British shipping in South America.

112
00:09:31,166 --> 00:09:40,161
In 1917 he was arrested in New York, faked paralysis in a hospital prison ward for nearly two years, and escaped.

113
00:09:40,610 --> 00:09:43,643
By 1937 he was back in touch with Ritter.

114
00:09:43,643 --> 00:09:49,485
When Sebold finally met him, Duquesne was immediately suspicious of the office.

115
00:09:49,485 --> 00:09:54,440
He passed Sebold a note suggesting they should talk somewhere else.

116
00:09:54,440 --> 00:10:00,651
The two men went to an automat, a coin-operated cafeteria, and talked there instead.

117
00:10:01,100 --> 00:10:04,779
The agents assigned to Duquesne found him hard work.

118
00:10:04,779 --> 00:10:13,057
Agent Raymond Newkirk rented the room above his apartment near Central Park and listened through a hidden microphone.

119
00:10:13,057 --> 00:10:17,302
Newkirk later described how Duquesne moved through the city.

120
00:10:17,751 --> 00:10:27,123
He would take a local train, change to an express, change back to a local, go through a revolving door and keep going right around.

121
00:10:27,123 --> 00:10:31,200
Duquesne even confronted one of the agents following him.

122
00:10:31,650 --> 00:10:34,810
And yet he kept coming back to Sawyer's office.

123
00:10:34,810 --> 00:10:37,970
That is the paradox at the center of this case.

124
00:10:37,970 --> 00:10:42,945
The most careful man in the network used the one channel that was not his.

125
00:10:43,394 --> 00:10:47,360
He brought photographs and specifications of a new bomb.

126
00:10:47,360 --> 00:10:51,680
He explained how fires could be started in industrial plants.

127
00:10:51,680 --> 00:10:55,575
He said he had slipped into a DuPont plant in Delaware.

128
00:10:55,575 --> 00:10:58,549
That is his claim, not a fact in the file.

129
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:03,340
Much of what he gathered came not from break-ins but from the mail.

130
00:11:03,340 --> 00:11:08,586
He wrote to companies and government offices as a writer and lecturer, and asked.

131
00:11:09,037 --> 00:11:14,392
One letter went to the Army's Chemical Warfare Service, about a new gas mask.

132
00:11:14,392 --> 00:11:23,573
He told them not to be concerned if the information was confidential, because it would be in the hands of a good, patriotic citizen.

133
00:11:25,224 --> 00:11:30,169
Through the office and the radio, the rest of the network came into view.

134
00:11:30,169 --> 00:11:33,082
It was not a ring of professional officers.

135
00:11:33,533 --> 00:11:39,730
It was mostly ordinary people with ordinary jobs, placed where secrets passed by.

136
00:11:39,730 --> 00:11:46,463
Ship's cooks, butchers, bakers and stewards who could carry letters across the Atlantic.

137
00:11:46,463 --> 00:11:47,611
A truck driver.

138
00:11:47,611 --> 00:11:48,529
A carpenter.

139
00:11:48,529 --> 00:11:49,371
A musician.

140
00:11:49,821 --> 00:11:54,661
Rene Mezenen was a steward on Pan American's transatlantic clipper.

141
00:11:54,661 --> 00:12:02,608
He could carry papers from New York to Lisbon in about a day, and he reported the convoys he saw from the air.

142
00:12:03,058 --> 00:12:09,723
He boasted to Sebold that he hid letters so well that finding them would mean taking the airplane apart.

143
00:12:10,173 --> 00:12:12,946
Everett Roeder was born in the Bronx.

144
00:12:12,946 --> 00:12:20,890
He was an engineer at the Sperry Gyroscope Company, working on confidential designs for the Army and Navy.

145
00:12:21,341 --> 00:12:31,878
According to the FBI, he passed on blueprints for the radio equipment of a new Glenn Martin bomber, range finders and blind-flying instruments.

146
00:12:31,878 --> 00:12:36,816
When agents searched his Long Island home, they found sixteen guns.

147
00:12:37,267 --> 00:12:39,558
Lilly Stein had come from Vienna.

148
00:12:39,558 --> 00:12:48,307
She had trained at the same Hamburg school as Sebold, and in New York she moved in social circles and gathered what she could.

149
00:12:48,307 --> 00:12:54,000
Her address was used by others as a return address for material bound for Germany.

150
00:12:54,451 --> 00:12:56,744
And the stakes were not abstract.

151
00:12:56,744 --> 00:13:11,269
Prosecutors later told the jury that Leo Waalen, a painter at a boatyard building small craft for the Navy, had handed in the sailing date of an American freighter, the Robin Moor, for transmission to Germany.

152
00:13:11,720 --> 00:13:17,918
In May 1941 a U-boat sank the Robin Moor in the Atlantic off West Africa.

153
00:13:17,918 --> 00:13:22,164
Everyone aboard survived, after days in lifeboats.

154
00:13:22,164 --> 00:13:27,089
The file does not show that his report caused the sinking.

155
00:13:27,538 --> 00:13:30,626
Some ideas were stopped in the office itself.

156
00:13:30,626 --> 00:13:41,328
A seaman named Erich Strunck asked for permission to steal the diplomatic bag of a British officer traveling on his ship, and to push the officer overboard.

157
00:13:41,778 --> 00:13:45,776
Sebold talked him out of it, telling him it was too risky.

158
00:13:45,776 --> 00:13:50,875
Another member, Paul Bante, brought dynamite and detonators to the office.

159
00:13:52,524 --> 00:13:53,886
Now, the umbrella.

160
00:13:53,886 --> 00:13:57,215
Hermann Lang was an inspector at the Carl L.

161
00:13:57,215 --> 00:14:06,141
Norden company, which made the Norden bombsight, one of the most closely guarded instruments in the American military.

162
00:14:06,141 --> 00:14:12,117
In 1937 Lang met Ritter in New York and told him he had access to the drawings.

163
00:14:12,567 --> 00:14:21,024
According to the FBI, Lang took the drawings home and copied them at his kitchen table at night, while his family slept.

164
00:14:21,475 --> 00:14:32,134
On January ninth, 1938, he handed the copies, hidden in the wooden casing of an umbrella, to a courier aboard a ship bound for Bremen.

165
00:14:32,134 --> 00:14:39,532
He was paid fifteen hundred dollars, and later invited to Germany to help rebuild the device.

166
00:14:39,980 --> 00:14:44,141
This is where historians are careful, and so is this file.

167
00:14:44,141 --> 00:14:46,005
How much did Germany gain?

168
00:14:46,456 --> 00:15:02,550
German bombsights of the war used a stabilized, gyroscopic design with similarities to the Norden, but the Luftwaffe also developed its own instruments, and there is no agreement that Lang's drawings changed the air war.

169
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,762
What is clear is the timing.

170
00:15:05,762 --> 00:15:12,865
The theft happened in 1937 and 1938, long before Sebold's first message.

171
00:15:13,315 --> 00:15:17,062
So the case did not prevent the ring's greatest coup.

172
00:15:17,062 --> 00:15:19,820
What it did was find the man behind it.

173
00:15:19,820 --> 00:15:25,548
Ritter, in Germany, still wanted Lang's help, and Sebold was told to contact him.

174
00:15:25,996 --> 00:15:32,615
Through the office, the FBI connected the bombsight theft to a living network in New York.

175
00:15:32,615 --> 00:15:36,660
Lang refused an offer to be taken to safety in Germany.

176
00:15:36,660 --> 00:15:38,720
He stayed at home in Queens.

177
00:15:40,370 --> 00:15:51,644
By the early summer of 1941, the Bureau had what it needed: films, recordings, intercepted letters and sixteen months of radio traffic.

178
00:15:52,092 --> 00:15:58,829
The FBI print of Duquesne in Sawyer's office is dated June twenty-fifth, 1941.

179
00:15:58,829 --> 00:16:05,307
A few days later, at the end of June, agents moved in on the whole network.

180
00:16:05,756 --> 00:16:09,112
Thirty-three people were arrested and charged.

181
00:16:09,112 --> 00:16:10,862
Nineteen pleaded guilty.

182
00:16:10,862 --> 00:16:19,833
A Japanese naval officer who had met Sebold, and been filmed doing it, slipped away to the West Coast and sailed for Tokyo.

183
00:16:19,833 --> 00:16:22,021
He was never tried in America.

184
00:16:22,471 --> 00:16:29,698
The other fourteen went on trial in Federal District Court in Brooklyn on September third, 1941.

185
00:16:29,698 --> 00:16:33,913
The jury watched the FBI's films from behind the mirror.

186
00:16:33,913 --> 00:16:36,547
They saw Duquesne at Sawyer's desk.

187
00:16:36,547 --> 00:16:43,473
They heard the men from the ships and the factories talking about convoys, bombs and plants.

188
00:16:43,923 --> 00:16:46,471
The trial ran through the autumn.

189
00:16:46,471 --> 00:16:55,586
On December seventh, 1941, Japan attacked Pearl Harbor, and within days the United States was at war with Germany too.

190
00:16:55,586 --> 00:16:59,834
On December thirteenth, the jury returned its verdicts.

191
00:16:59,834 --> 00:17:01,765
All fourteen were guilty.

192
00:17:02,216 --> 00:17:06,736
On January second, 1942, the sentences came down.

193
00:17:06,736 --> 00:17:11,073
Duquesne received eighteen years for espionage.

194
00:17:11,073 --> 00:17:12,180
So did Lang.

195
00:17:12,180 --> 00:17:14,486
Roeder was given sixteen.

196
00:17:14,486 --> 00:17:15,962
Lilly Stein ten.

197
00:17:16,413 --> 00:17:19,854
Together, more than three hundred years in prison.

198
00:17:19,854 --> 00:17:27,770
A German intelligence officer would later call the roundup the death blow to German espionage in the United States.

199
00:17:28,221 --> 00:17:35,310
Hoover called it the greatest spy roundup in American history, and the Bureau told the story often.

200
00:17:35,310 --> 00:17:42,544
Four years later Hollywood told it too, as The House on Ninety-Second Street, with the names changed.

201
00:17:42,994 --> 00:17:46,126
The FBI's version made the Bureau the hero.

202
00:17:46,126 --> 00:17:50,640
Hold on to that as well, because it shaped who was remembered.

203
00:17:51,090 --> 00:17:55,417
There is a consequence that connects this case to another file.

204
00:17:55,417 --> 00:18:01,873
With its networks in America gone, the Abwehr could no longer rely on agents already in place.

205
00:18:02,323 --> 00:18:10,089
In June 1942, it sent saboteurs instead, landed by U-boat on Long Island and in Florida.

206
00:18:10,089 --> 00:18:14,413
That operation, Pastorius, is a story of its own.

207
00:18:14,413 --> 00:18:19,356
But it began with the empty space this case left behind.

208
00:18:21,005 --> 00:18:24,863
Duquesne served his sentence at Leavenworth, in Kansas.

209
00:18:24,863 --> 00:18:30,827
According to the accounts of his life, he was mistreated and beaten by other inmates.

210
00:18:31,277 --> 00:18:37,353
In 1954, in poor health, he was released, after about fourteen years.

211
00:18:37,353 --> 00:18:45,542
He died penniless at City Hospital on Welfare Island in New York, on May twenty-fourth, 1956.

212
00:18:45,542 --> 00:18:47,391
He was seventy-eight.

213
00:18:47,843 --> 00:18:49,197
Sebold disappeared.

214
00:18:49,197 --> 00:18:57,110
He was given a new identity and moved to California, where he tried, among other things, to run a chicken farm.

215
00:18:57,559 --> 00:19:00,749
He struggled to hold a job and was often ill.

216
00:19:00,749 --> 00:19:07,765
Letters from Germany suggested that people there still wanted revenge, and he lived with that fear.

217
00:19:08,215 --> 00:19:16,008
In 1965 he was committed to Napa State Hospital, diagnosed with manic depression.

218
00:19:16,008 --> 00:19:22,261
He died there on February sixteenth, 1970, at the age of seventy.

219
00:19:22,711 --> 00:19:25,725
A book had told his story in 1943.

220
00:19:25,725 --> 00:19:36,714
It was not until 2014 that a full history, by the journalist Peter Duffy, called him the first hero of the Second World War.

221
00:19:37,164 --> 00:19:42,271
Here is the final judgment of this file, and it is an interpretation.

222
00:19:42,271 --> 00:19:48,266
The famous version of this case is about the FBI's skill, and the skill was real.

223
00:19:48,717 --> 00:19:58,591
But the decisive act came first, and it came from one frightened man in Cologne who walked into a consulate when he could have stayed silent.

224
00:19:58,591 --> 00:20:02,934
Without that visit, there is no office, no radio and no trial.

225
00:20:03,384 --> 00:20:05,654
What the case changed was method.

226
00:20:05,654 --> 00:20:17,898
It showed that the best way to fight a spy service was not only to arrest its agents, but to become its channel: to control what it heard and to let it lead you to everyone else.

227
00:20:18,348 --> 00:20:30,346
In Britain, MI5 was running the same idea at the same time, and on a far larger scale, turning captured German agents into what became the Double Cross system.

228
00:20:30,797 --> 00:20:38,239
One of its first double agents, a Welshman code-named SNOW, had also been recruited by Major Ritter.

229
00:20:38,239 --> 00:20:44,715
The same spymaster was beaten twice, on both sides of the Atlantic, by the same method.

230
00:20:45,164 --> 00:20:47,600
Thirty-three people were convicted.

231
00:20:47,600 --> 00:20:54,210
Much of what they handed over after Sebold's arrival went through a channel the FBI controlled.

232
00:20:54,210 --> 00:21:00,959
And the one secret that mattered most had escaped before anyone was watching, inside an umbrella.

233
00:21:01,411 --> 00:21:02,984
This file is closed.

234
00:21:02,984 --> 00:21:07,628
New files are declassified every day on The Defector Files.
